<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1823 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hymn to Liberty</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="el">Ύμνος εις την Ελευθερίαν</bdi> / <bdi lang="el-Latn">Ýmnos eis tin Eleftherían</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A long poem of 1823 about the Greeks' fight for freedom; its first two verses are the anthem of Greece and of Cyprus.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Dionysios Solomos</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Nikolaos Mantzaros</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Greeks; later also Greek Cypriots</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Zakynthos, Greece</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem 1823; music 1828</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Dionysios Solomos (1823); music by Nikolaos Mantzaros (1828))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Dionysios Solomos wrote the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hymn-to-liberty">Hymn to Liberty</a>&quot;, also called the &quot;Hymn to Freedom&quot;, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> in 1823, inspired by the Greek War of Independence, to honour the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>' struggle for independence after centuries of Ottoman rule. The poem runs to 158 stanzas. It was printed in Messolonghi the following year, and in October 1824 the Philhellenic Committee published it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, while an Italian translation appeared at about the same time in the Messolonghi newspaper Ellinika Chronika.</p><p>In 1828 the Corfiot operatic composer Nikolaos Mantzaros set it to music as a choral work in 24 parts and dedicated it to Otto, the first King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Otto awarded him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> Cross of the Order of the Redeemer, but during Otto's reign (1832–1862) the anthem in use was one based on &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;, with words glorifying the king.</p><p>After Otto was ousted in 1862, the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hymn-to-liberty">Hymn to Liberty</a>&quot; was adopted in 1864 as the national and royal anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, its first two stanzas forming the anthem. It was the royal anthem from 1864 to 1924 and again from 1935 to 1973, ending each time with the abolition of the monarchy. An adapted version served as the anthem of the short-lived Cretan State.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, an anthem written for the Republic by the Cypriot composer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a> Michaelides and shared with the Turkish Cypriots was abandoned in 1963 during intercommunal conflicts, and in 1966 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-european-union">Council of Ministers</a> adopted the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hymn-to-liberty">Hymn to Liberty</a>&quot; as the national anthem of Cyprus. The anthem is performed at every closing ceremony of the Olympic Games in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> as the birthplace of the Games, usually without words; it was sung at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Vancouver. Military bands play an arrangement by Lieutenant Colonel Margaritis Kastellis (1907–1979), a former director of the Greek Music Corps.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The poem tells of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>' suffering under Ottoman rule and their hope of freedom, and follows events of the War of Independence: the execution of Patriarch Gregory V, the response of the Great Powers, at length the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli-greece">Tripolitsa</a>, and the Christian character of the struggle. The mood is exalted and martial.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Greek</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Greek</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Σὲ γνωρίζω ἀπὸ τὴν κόψι</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Se gnorízo apó tin kópsi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I know you by the edge</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">τοῦ σπαθιοῦ τὴν τρομερή,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">tou spathioú tin tromerí,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of the sword, the fearsome one,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">σὲ γνωρίζω ἀπὸ τὴν ὄψι</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">se gnorízo apó tin ópsi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I know you by the look</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ποὺ μὲ βία μετράει τὴν γῆ.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">pou me vía metráei tin gi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that measures the earth with force.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ἀπ’ τὰ κόκαλα βγαλμένη</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Ap' ta kókala vgalméni</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Brought forth from the bones,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">τῶν Ἑλλήνων τὰ ἱερά,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">ton Ellínon ta ierá,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sacred ones, of the Greeks,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ σὰν πρῶτα ἀνδρειωμένη,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai san próta andreioméni,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and valiant as at first,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">χαῖρε, ὦ χαῖρε, Ἐλευθεριά!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">chaíre, o chaíre, Eleftheriá!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>hail, O hail, Liberty!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ἐκεῖ μέσα ἐκατοικοῦσες</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Ekeí mésa ekatoikoúses</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In there you dwelt,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">πικραμένη, ἐντροπαλή,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">pikraméni, entropalí,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>embittered, shamefaced,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">κι ἕνα στόμα ἀκαρτεροῦσες,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">ki éna stóma akarteroúses,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and you waited for a mouth</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">«ἔλα πάλι», νά σοῦ πῇ.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">«éla páli», na sou pi.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to say to you, «come again».</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ἄργειε νά'λθῃ ἐκείνη ἡ μέρα,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Árgeie ná'lthi ekeíni i méra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That day was slow to come,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">κι ἦταν ὅλα σιωπηλά,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">ki ítan óla siopilá,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and all was silent,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">γιατὶ τὰ ’σκιαζε ἡ φοβέρα</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">giatí ta 'skiaze i fovéra</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>because threat cowed them</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ τὰ πλάκωνε ἡ σκλαβιά.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai ta plákone i sklaviá.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and slavery weighed them down.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 96 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Δυστυχής! Παρηγορία</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Dystychís! Parigoría</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Unhappy one! The only consolation</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">μόνη σοῦ ἕμενε νὰ λὲς</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">móni sou émene na les</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>left to you was to tell of</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">περασμένα μεγαλεία</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">perasména megaleía</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>past glories</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ διηγώντας τὰ νὰ κλαῖς.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai diigóntas ta na klais.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and, telling of them, to weep.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Καὶ ἀκαρτέρει καὶ ἀκαρτέρει</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Kai akartérei kai akartérei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And waited and waited</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">φιλελεύθερη λαλιά,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">fileléftheri laliá,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for a liberty-loving voice,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἕνα ἐκτύπαε τ’ ἄλλο χέρι</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">éna ektýpae t' állo chéri</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>one hand struck the other</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἀπὸ τὴν ἀπελπισιά,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">apó tin apelpisiá,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>out of despair,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Κι ἔλεες: «Πότε, ἅ, πότε βγάνω</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Ki élees: «Póte, a, póte vgáno</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And you said: «When, ah, when shall I lift</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">τὸ κεφάλι ἀπὸ τσ’ ἐρμιές;».</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">to kefáli apó ts' ermiés?»</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>my head out of the desolate places?»</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Καὶ ἀποκρίνοντο ἀπὸ πάνω</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Kai apokrínonto apó páno</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And there answered from above</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">κλάψες, ἄλυσες, φωνές.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">klápses, ályses, fonés.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>laments, chains, cries.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Τότε ἐσήκωνες τὸ βλέμμα</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Tóte esíkones to vlémma</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Then you raised your gaze,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">μὲς στὰ κλάιματα θολό,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">mes sta kláimata tholó,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>clouded in tears,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ εἰς τὸ ροῦχο σου ἔσταζ’ αἷμα,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai eis to roúcho sou éstaz' aíma,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and on your garment blood dripped,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">πλῆθος αἷμα ἑλληνικό.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">plíthos aíma ellinikó.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a multitude of Greek blood.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Μὲ τὰ ροῦχα αἱματωμένα</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Me ta roúcha aimatoména</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With your clothes bloodied</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ξέρω ὅτι ἔβγαινες κρυφὰ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">xéro óti évgaines kryfá</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I know that you went out in secret</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">νὰ γυρεύῃς εἰς τὰ ξένα</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">na gyrévis eis ta xéna</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to seek in foreign lands</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἄλλα χέρια δυνατά.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">álla chéria dynatá.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>other, strong hands.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Μοναχὴ τὸ δρόμο ἐπῆρες,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Monachí to drómo epíres,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Alone you took the road,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἐξανάλθες μοναχή·</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">exanálthes monachí;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>alone you came back again;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">δὲν εἴν' εὔκολες οἱ θύρες</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">den eín' éfkoles oi thýres</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>doors are not easy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἐὰν ἡ χρεία τὲς κουρταλῇ.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">eán i chreía tes kourtalí.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>if need knocks on them.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ἄλλός σοῦ ἔκλαψε εἰς τὰ στήθια,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Állos sou éklapse eis ta stíthia,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One wept on your breast,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἀλλ' ἀνάσασι καμμιά·</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">all' anásasi kammiá;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>but no relief at all;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἄλλος σου ἔταξε βοήθεια</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">állos sou étaxe voítheia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>another promised you help</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ σὲ γέλασε φρικτά.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai se gélase friktá.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and deceived you horribly.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ἄλλοι, ὀϊμέ, στὴ συμφορά σου</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Álloi, oïmé, sti symforá sou</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Others, alas, at your misfortune</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ὀποῦ ἐχαίροντο πολύ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">opoú echaíronto polý,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who rejoiced greatly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">«σύρε νὰ 'βρῃς τὰ παιδιά σου,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">«sýre na 'vris ta paidiá sou,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>«go and find your children,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">σύρε», ἔλεγαν οἱ σκληροί.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">sýre», élegan oi skliroí.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>go», said the cruel ones.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Φεύγει ὀπίσω τὸ ποδάρι</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Févgei opíso to podári</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The foot flees backward</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ ὀλογλήγορο πατεῖ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai ologlígoro pateí</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and very swiftly treads</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἢ τὴν πέτρα ἢ τὸ χορτάρι</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">í tin pétra í to chortári</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>either the stone or the grass</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ποὺ τὴ δόξα σοῦ ἐνθυμεῖ.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">pou ti dóxa sou enthymeí.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that reminds you of your glory.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ταπεινότατή σου γέρνει</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Tapeinótatí sou gérnei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Most humbly droops</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἡ τρισάθλια κεφαλή,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">i trisáthlia kefalí,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your thrice-wretched head,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">σὰν πτωχοῦ ποὺ θυροδέρνει</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">san ptochoú pou thyrodérnei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>like a pauper's who knocks from door to door</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">κι εἶναι βάρος του ἡ ζωή.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">ki eínai város tou i zoí.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and whose life is a burden to him.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ναί, ἀλλὰ τώρα ἀντιπαλεύει</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Naí, allá tóra antipalévei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Yes, but now there fights back</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">κάθε τέκνο σου μὲ ὁρμή,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">káthe tékno sou me ormí,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>every child of yours with vigour,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ποὺ ἀκατάπαυστα γυρεύει</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">pou akatápafsta gyrévei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who ceaselessly seeks</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἢ τὴ νίκη ἢ τὴ θανῆ.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">í ti níki í ti thaní.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>either victory or death.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ἀπ' τὰ κόκαλα βγαλμένη</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Ap' ta kókala vgalméni</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Brought forth from the bones,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">τῶν Ἑλλήνων τὰ ἱερά,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">ton Ellínon ta ierá,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sacred ones, of the Greeks,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ σὰν πρῶτα ἀνδρειωμένη,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai san próta andreioméni,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and valiant as at first,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">χαῖρε, ὦ χαῖρε, Ἐλευθεριά!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">chaíre, o chaíre, Eleftheriá!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>hail, O hail, Liberty!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Μόλις εἶδε τὴν ὁρμή σου</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Mólis eíde tin ormí sou</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As soon as it saw your onrush,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ὁ οὐρανὸς ποὺ γιὰ τσ' ἐχθροὺς</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">o ouranós pou gia ts' echthroús</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sky, which for the enemies</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">εἰς τὴ γῆ τὴ μητρική σου</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">eis ti gi ti mitrikí sou</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in your mother earth</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἔτρεφ' ἄνθια καὶ καρπούς,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">étref' ánthia kai karpoús,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>nourished flowers and fruits,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἐγαλήνεψε· καὶ ἐχύθει</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">egalínepse; kai echýthei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>grew calm; and there poured forth</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καταχθόνια μιὰ βοή,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">katachthónia mia voí,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a roar from beneath the earth,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ τοῦ Ρήγα σοῦ ἀπεκρίθη</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai tou Ríga sou apekríthi</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and of Rigas there answered you</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">πολεμόκραχτη ἡ φωνή.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">polemókrachti i foní.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the war-shouting voice.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ὅλοι οἱ τόποι σου σ' ἐκράξαν</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Óloi oi tópoi sou s' ekráxan</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All your lands called out to you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">χαιρετώντας σὲ θερμά,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">chairetóntas se thermá,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>greeting you warmly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ τὰ στόματα ἐφωνάξαν</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai ta stómata efonáxan</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the mouths cried out</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ὅσα αἰσθάνετο ἡ καρδιά.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">ósa aistháneto i kardiá.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>all that the heart felt.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ἐφωνάξανε ὣς τ' ἀστέρια</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Efonáxane os t' astéria</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They cried out to the stars,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">τοῦ Ἰονίου καὶ τὰ νησιά,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">tou Ioníou kai ta nisiá,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the islands of the Ionian too,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">κι ἐσηκώσανε τὰ χέρια</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">ki esikósane ta chéria</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and raised their hands</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">γιὰ νὰ δείξουνε χαρά,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">gia na deíxoune chará,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to show joy,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">μ' ὅλον ποὺ 'ναι ἀλυσωμένο</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">m' ólon pou 'nai alysoméno</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>although it is chained,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">τὸ καθένα τεχνικά,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">to kathéna techniká,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>each one, cunningly,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ εἰς τὸ μέτωπο γραμμένο</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai eis to métopo gramméno</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and on its forehead written</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ἔχει: «Ψεύτρα Ἐλευθεριά».</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">échei: «Pséftra Eleftheriá».</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it has: «False Liberty».</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Γκαρδιακὰ χαροποιήθει</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Gkardiaká charopoiíthei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Heartily rejoiced</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ τοῦ Βάσιγκτον ἡ γῆ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai tou Vásigkton i gi,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the land of Washington too,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ τὰ σίδερα ἐνθυμήθει</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai ta sídera enthymíthei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and remembered the irons</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">ποὺ τὴν ἔδεναν κι αὐτή.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">pou tin édenan ki aftí.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that bound it also.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ἀπ' τὸν πύργο τοῦ φωνάζει,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Ap' ton pýrgo tou fonázei,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From its tower it cries out,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">σὰ νὰ λέῃ σὲ χαιρετῶ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">sa na léi se chairetó,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as if to say, I greet you,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">καὶ τὴ χήτη τοῦ τινάζει</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">kai ti chíti tou tinázei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and shakes its mane,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">τὸ λιοντάρι τὸ Ἱσπανό.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">to liontári to Ispanó.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the Spanish lion.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">Ἐλαφιάσθη τῆς Ἀγγλίας</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">Elafiásthi tis Anglías</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Startled like a deer was England's</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">τὸ θηρίο, καὶ σέρνει εὐθὺς</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">to thirío, kai sérnei efthýs</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>beast, and at once it sends</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">κατὰ τ' ἄκρα τῆς Ρουσίας</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">katá t' ákra tis Rousías</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>towards the ends of Russia</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="el">τὰ μουγκρίσματα τσ' ὀργῆς.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="el-Latn">ta mougkrísmata ts' orgís.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the roars of its wrath.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Dionysios Solomos (1823); the first 24 of its 158 stanzas. Text from <a href="https://el.wikisource.org/wiki/%CE%8E%CE%BC%CE%BD%CE%BF%CF%82_%CE%B5%CE%B9%CF%82_%CF%84%CE%B7%CE%BD_%CE%95%CE%BB%CE%B5%CF%85%CE%B8%CE%B5%CF%81%CE%AF%CE%B1%CE%BD" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ELOT 743).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (15)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: ELOT 743 transliteration of the spelling as printed (polytonic marks reduced to the stress accent, breathings and iota subscripts dropped; monosyllables unaccented except 'í', 'or'); the Greek question mark ';' and raised point '·' are given as '?' and ';'.</li><li>line 1.1: 'κόψι' is the cutting edge of the sword, or its cut, its stroke.</li><li>line 1.3: 'ὄψι' is the look, face or appearance.</li><li>line 3.1: 'In there': in the bones of the Greeks (stanza 2), where Liberty dwelt while Greece was enslaved.</li><li>line 4.3: 'σκιάζω' (Heptanesian) is 'to frighten, cow'; 'φοβέρα' is threat or intimidation.</li><li>line 6.1: 'ἀκαρτέρει' is read as continuing the second person of stanza 3 ('[you] waited and waited'); grammatically it can also be a third person, with 'a liberty-loving voice' as the one waiting. Unsure.</li><li>line 7.2: 'ἐρμιές' are desolate, lonely places (Heptanesian 'ερημιές'); 'τσ'' is the Heptanesian article 'τις'.</li><li>line 10.4: 'κουρταλῇ' (Heptanesian) = knocks.</li><li>line 13.1: the foot is Liberty's: she turns back and treads swiftly on any stone or grass that reminds her of her former glory.</li><li>line 14.3: 'θυροδέρνει' = goes begging from door to door, literally 'beats on doors'.</li><li>line 15.4: 'θανῆ' (Heptanesian) = death.</li><li>line 18.3: Rigas Feraios (Velestinlis), the revolutionary poet executed in 1798.</li><li>lines 20.1-21.4: the Ionian Islands, then a British protectorate: each is chained 'cunningly' ('τεχνικά', with art) and bears 'False Liberty' on its brow.</li><li>line 23.1: the lion on a tower: the arms of Castile and León; Spain was then in revolution (1820-1823).</li><li>line 24.1: 'Ἐλαφιάσθη' = was startled, shied (from 'ἐλάφι', deer); line 24.2 'σέρνει' = drags, here sends forth.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Italian translation, Ellinika Chronika (Messolonghi)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1824</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Choral setting in 24 parts (Nikolaos Mantzaros)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1828</span><span class="sg-chain-h">musical setting, dedicated to King Otto</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Cretan Anthem</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted version, anthem of the Cretan State</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Military band arrangement (Margaritis Kastellis)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li></ul></section>
